{# DECIDED: traefik's fence is an ipBlock origin lock on :8443, a from-less :8000 and a
   cross-namespace prometheus grant on :8080, so it stays bespoke.
   The callers model (`netpol_from` / `netpol_fences`, macro in
   k8s/templates/networkpolicy.yml.j2) renders one TCP port admitting same-namespace `app:`
   peers, and its header rules out growing it to absorb another shape.
#}
---
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: traefik
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: traefik
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# THE ROLLBACK LEVER. Every bespoke policy in this role carries this same conditional, and this
   file carries the reasoning the others point back to.

   `netpol_baseline_enforced` is the documented undo, and it only works if each bespoke policy
   renders allow-all when the flag is false. `kubectl apply` does not prune, so skipping a
   policy's render would leave the applied policy live and still fencing, minus the
   traefik/prometheus grants the flag also switches off -- strictly worse than not rolling back
   at all. Real deletion is not available either; `kubectl delete networkpolicy` is deny-listed
   for agents and refused by the readonly SA. The observability policies
   (networkpolicy-observability.yaml.j2) use the same render-allow-all pattern.

   `| bool` matches `netpol_baseline_probe_active` in defaults/main.yml. Without it the two
   disagree for a STRING: `'false'` is truthy to Jinja (this branch renders, fencing applied) and
   False to that list (the verify Job dials no leg) -- enforcement with no probe. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_enforced | bool %}
    # The front door, in two rules because the two ports admit different sources.
    #
    # THE PORT NUMBERS HERE ARE POD PORTS, NOT SERVICE PORTS. A NetworkPolicy matches the
    # destination port on the POD, after kube-proxy has already DNAT'd the Service port to the
    # targetPort. traefik's Service maps 80 -> targetPort `http` and 443 -> `https`, and those
    # container ports are 8000 and 8443. Writing 80/443 here admits traffic to two ports nothing
    # listens on, and denies every in-cluster caller of the real ones; external traffic still
    # works, because it arrives over the VIP path rather than the ClusterIP.
    #
    # :8000 stays open to every source. It is the http->https redirect entrypoint
    # (static-config.yaml.j2, entryPoint.to: ":443") -- every client that arrives on plain HTTP
    # hits that redirect before reaching :8443 -- and the netpol probe's control legs dial
    # traefik:80 to prove the probe itself has a network. Nothing on :8000 reaches a backend, so
    # an origin-direct client gets a redirect to a port the next rule fences.
    - ports:
        - port: 8000
          protocol: TCP
    # :8443 admits only netpol_baseline_traefik_https_sources (defaults/main.yml): Cloudflare's
    # published ranges, the LAN, the WireGuard client range, and the pod CIDR. The only source
    # supposed to reach a public `Host(x.<domain>)` Rule is Cloudflare's edge, and its ranges
    # are already enumerated in `cloudflare_ips` for forwardedHeaders.trustedIPs. An origin-direct
    # client -- anyone who resolves the origin IP and sends the public Host header -- would skip
    # Cloudflare's WAF and bot rules, arrive with no trusted X-Forwarded-For, and share ONE
    # rate-limit bucket keyed on "" with every other origin-direct request. This list is the
    # origin lock. Authenticated Origin Pulls sits on top of it: the `cloudflare-origin-pull`
    # TLSOption (k8s/templates/origin-pull.yml.j2) requires Cloudflare's client certificate on
    # every public Host router, so a source this list admits still fails the handshake unless
    # the connection is Cloudflare's.
    #
    # ETP=Local preserves the source address on the VIP path, so each ipBlock matches what the
    # client really is. A WireGuard client arrives in one of two shapes, and both are listed:
    # as the Pi's LAN address if wg-easy's PostUp masquerades `wg_client_subnet` (the Pi's
    # docker-compose.yml.j2 sets no WG_POST_UP, so the image default decides), or as its own
    # 10.8.0.x address if it routes.
    #
    # The pod CIDR here is the WHOLE /16 on purpose, which docs/networkpolicy-default-deny.md
    # ("Correction carried into this design") forbids for the baseline. There a /16 readmits
    # every pod to a fenced workload and cancels the default-deny. Here every pod is a legitimate
    # caller: traefik is the ingress, and an in-cluster caller of a `.local.` route
    # (monitor-bridge, homepage's widgets) reaches the VIP as its own 10.42.x.y address, so
    # in-cluster reachability is unchanged and the rule is a strict narrowing. Both cni0
    # gateways and both flannel.1 addresses sit inside the /16, so host-originated callers (the
    # three host crons that dial traefik, a cross-node hop) need no separate /32 grant.
    #
    # The list carries only the IPv4 half of `cloudflare_ips`. kube-router buckets ipBlocks per
    # address family (evalIPBlockPeer in pkg/controllers/netpol/policy.go, v2.6) and installs an
    # ipset only for a family the node runs, so on this single-stack v4 cluster an IPv6 entry
    # fences nothing, and kube-router logs a warning per IPv6 CIDR on every policy sync. The
    # drift check compares `cloudflare_ips` itself against upstream, not this list, so dropping
    # the v6 half here hides no drift. A dual-stack cluster needs the filter in defaults/main.yml
    # removed, or every IPv6 client of a public name is refused at this port.
    - from:
{% for cidr in netpol_baseline_traefik_https_sources %}
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: {{ cidr }}
{% endfor %}
      ports:
        - port: 8443
          protocol: TCP
    # :8080 is the port worth fencing. prometheus runs in the observability namespace, so this is
    # cross-namespace: namespaceSelector and podSelector are siblings in ONE from item, which
    # ANDs them. As separate items they would OR, admitting every pod in observability and every
    # prometheus anywhere.
    - from:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: observability
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: prometheus
      ports:
        - port: 8080
          protocol: TCP
    # The crowdsec-agent SIDECAR's :6060 grant is not here: networkpolicy-crowdsec-sidecars.yaml.j2
    # renders it for every pod in crowdsec_k8s_sidecar_agents.
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
